## Step 6: Examine Memory Addresses

Now that we know the memory addresses, we can examine what’s stored at those addresses.

For example, the addresses in this program are:

* `0xffffe030`
* `0xffffe034`

In this output:

* `year` is stored at the lower address.
* `points` is stored 4 bytes later.

<img width="375" height="31" alt="image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/598788d9-d40d-43e9-8516-4c2e70dd0d14" />

---

## Step 7: Dump Memory Contents

To dump memory starting from the lower address (`0xffffe034`, or `year`):

* The command outputs three 32-bit values in hexadecimal starting at address `0x7efff1d0`.
* Example (from the book):

```
0x7efff1d0: 0x000007e4 0x0000001b 0x00000000
```

* Here:

  * `0x000007e4` = 2020 (decimal) → `year`
  * `0x0000001b` = 27 (decimal) → `points`
  * The next value is 0 and not one of our variables.

<img width="712" height="63" alt="image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/7700c256-17cb-42de-be7d-38ff93fe5025" />

---

## Step 8: View Memory in Decimal

Memory is usually examined in hexadecimal. To view it in decimal, use a different gdb command:

<img width="412" height="58" alt="image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/ddd8062c-f3f3-419e-868d-e25715c32047" />

* Variables are 32-bit (4-byte) chunks.
* Memory is byte-addressable, meaning each byte has its own address.
* `points` is 4 bytes after `year`.

---

### Step 9: Examine Byte-Level Memory

Looking at memory as a series of bytes:

<img width="927" height="87" alt="image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/6afdda03-25c1-4fca-aa2f-e442b4443f07" />

* The value for `year` is emphasized.
* Least significant byte (`0xe4`) comes first due to **little-endian data storage**.

